  "limitations": [
    "The intent corpus is largely machine-generated; real customer typos, dialects and disfluencies are under-represented.",
    "Gold intent labels are trusted from the source; the escalate flag and alt_intent are machine-assisted annotations, and escalate reflects a policy, not ground truth.",
    "Scam training/test data is SMS-style, not shop-chat DMs.",
    "The in-distribution test set shares a source with the training data; the OOD result is the more conservative number.",
    "The inbox is simulated; no live Facebook integration.",
    "Class \"Delivery\" includes pre-purchase delivery questions.",
    "Auto-lane coverage is 53.6% at tau=0.85, not the 60% an earlier draft of docs/07 §5 asked for, with 94.2% accuracy on the slice that is handled. That target predates any measurement and is unreachable while messages that ask for a person are never auto-answered: a perfect escalate oracle still leaves coverage at 56.2%. The target was corrected to the measured operating point instead of the system being bent toward the target.",
    "Second-signal systems (B2, E2) were measured with the LLM reachable.",
    "OOD (BanglaTOD e-commerce, Bangla utterances): exact 6-class accuracy 36.2% on n=105, against a coarse 10->6 intent mapping that is a judgement call.",
    "Scam false alarms on ordinary D1 shop chat: 0.25% at SCAM_T=0.7.",
    "D2 'promo' was excluded from scam training: inspection showed marketing offers and political propaganda, not fraud.",
    "Engine A in this run: tfidf-lr (Laya candidate adopted: False).",
    "Reported latency was measured locally on CPU; re-measure on the deployed Space (docs/07 §4)."
